WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.509
Pixar's super-family the Incredibles are back
in a long-awaited sequel that's bursting with

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:08.160
easter eggs, references and secret cameos.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.769
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm breaking down the top 30 Pixar,

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:16.680
superhero, and pop culture easter eggs you
might have missed in Incredibles 2 and what

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.660
that post-credits teaser means for Incredibles
3.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.080
I'm also starting a new Incredibles giveaway;
you just need to be a subscriber and leave

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.340
a comment below about the movie to enter.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.109
Spoilers ahead, so take care if you haven't
seen the film yet.

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:35.010
A113 is a regular Pixar easter egg that's
an homage to the California Institute of the

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.839
Arts classroom where many of Pixar's animators
studied, and it pops up multiple times in

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:41.159
Incredibles 2.

00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:44.500
It appears on the runaway monorail that Elastigirl
stops.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.980
It's also the number of the room where Elastigirl
watches the Screenslaver's broadcast.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.180
It's also on the side of the hydroliner where
the final act takes place.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.839
And A-1-1-3 also shows up at the movie theatre
at the end of the film and it's sneakily hidden

00:00:57.839 --> 00:01:00.860
in the movie posters for Dementia 113.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.470
By the way, the film playing at the movie
theatre is also a reference to an early 1960s

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.110
Francis Ford Coppola movie.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:11.300
Edna Mode and Jack-Jack's scenes are some
of the best parts of the sequel.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.210
When Bob goes to Edna's place where she's
been babysitting Jack-Jack, she tells him

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:19.450
that the super-powered toddler likes Mozart,
which is a nod to Kari the babysitter who

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.560
mentioned Mozart in the first movie and also
used his music in the short film, Jack-Jack

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:24.560
Attack.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.950
"I also brought Mozart to play while he sleeps
because leading experts say Mozart makes babies

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.950
smarter."

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.420
There's a very clever Poltergeist reference
during the Jack-Jack cookie scene.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.710
Craig T. Nelson, who starred in the classic
1982 horror movie and who's also the voice

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:42.570
of Mr Incredible, hears Jack-Jack talking
from another dimension after he disappears

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.330
leaving a remote control behind.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:45.450
"What the..?!"

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:46.450
"Cookie!

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.810
Cha cha want a cookie?!"

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.490
"Nam nam cookie!"

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.290
"Cha cha want num num?"

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:57.670
In Poltergeist, one of Craig T. Nelson's children
gets sucked into another dimension by ghosts

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.860
and, similar to Jack-Jack, the family can
hear her voice from the other dimension in

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.860
their TV set.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.040
"Where are you?!"

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.110
"Mummy!"

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.060
The Pizza Planet truck from the first Toy
Story movie is a regular Pixar easter egg

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.810
which has appeared in every movie so far,
the only exception being the first Incredibles.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:20.740
This time around though, a period-styled Pizza
Planet truck was specially designed to fit

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.390
the setting of the sequel.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:26.990
This easter egg was placed by sets supervisor
Nathan Fariss who notoriously made the Pizza

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.410
Planet truck in Finding Dory almost impossible
to find.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:35.010
In Incredibles 2 though the Screenslaver is
revealed as being an ordinary pizza guy so

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.900
the Pizza Planet truck is likely in a scene
he appears in.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.880
Another regular easter egg is the Chinese
take-out container.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.230
This style of box has a very specific design
that recurs throughout Pixar's films after

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:51.010
first having appeared in A Bug's Life where
it was called the "Chinese Cabinet of Metamorphosis"

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.690
and used by Manny for his circus act.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:58.590
Another easter egg which supports the Pixar
theory of a shared universe is a billboard

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.720
in Incredibles 2 for TripleDent Gum which
is really hard to spot but it flashes by during

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.099
the Elastigirl train rescue scene.

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:09.090
TripleDent Gum is the brand of chewing gum
from Inside Out the jingle for which plays

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.360
throughout that movie.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.360
"TripleDent gum.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.780
Will make you smile"
"The song from the gum commercial?"

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.930
And there was also a TripleDent Gum easter
egg last year in Cars 3 with the brand appearing

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:22.270
as a car sponsor.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:26.380
The recurring easter eggs of brands like Pizza
Planet and TripleDent Gum, as well as the

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:31.220
same Chinese takeout box design lend support
to the Pixar theory that all their movies

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:33.410
exist within the same universe.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:37.780
The mind-erasing device that Rick Dicker uses
to make Tony Rydinger forget Violet's secret

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.980
identity is the same one he used to erase
Kari's memory of the baby's superpowers in

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.840
Jack-Jack Attack.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.560
Incredibles 2, of course, includes lots of
call-backs to moments and details from the

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.650
first movie.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.300
When Mr Incredible and Elastigirl go to their
meeting with Devtech, they wear the original

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.849
costumes they had before Edna made their iconic
red suits.

00:03:55.849 --> 00:04:00.850
And Elastigirl's new silver suit and Elasticycle
both have an upgraded version of the logo

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:02.970
she used to have when she was a solo superheroine.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:03.970
Designers were strategic when it came to Helen's
multiple supersuits, building one basic suit

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:04.970
and varying the shading and logos to create
three distinct looks.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:05.970
The strategy required some tweaks to Elastigirl's
original suit, shrinking and moving her logo

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:06.970
so that it fit within the established template.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:08.370
Elastigirl's darker, sleeker design is meant
to be practical to help sneak around in the

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:12.810
shadows tracking villains, however, when Helen
complains that it's dark and angsty, she's

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:17.209
also commenting on modern superhero movies
which have tended to shy away from bright

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:18.209
colourful costumes.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:22.870
"I'm not all dark and angsty, I'm Elastigirl."

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:27.020
At the Incredible family's new house, there's
a mug with a logo for Insuricare, Bob's old

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.879
employer from the first movie.

00:04:28.879 --> 00:04:33.939
The waterfall that Elastigirl drives the Elasticycle
out of is reminiscent of Syndrome's lava waterfall

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:37.349
which also worked as a hidden opening in the
first Incredibles movie.

00:04:37.349 --> 00:04:40.949
Plus, I also got some Batcave vibes from this
scene.

00:04:40.949 --> 00:04:45.430
And when Elastigirl uses her body as a parachute
to slow down the speeding monorail, it's a

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:49.610
call-back to when she performed a similar
move on the monorail at Syndrome's island

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:50.610
hideout.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:53.940
The other big regular Pixar easter egg is
the famous Luxo ball.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.589
If you want to spot that in this movie, look
out for Jack-Jack's cradle.

00:04:57.589 --> 00:05:02.500
The Luxo ball appeared in Pixar's very first
short film, Luxo Jr, together with two desk

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.400
lamps, one of which is still used in Pixar's
logo.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:09.629
As well as all the Pixar references, there
are also some cool superhero easter eggs and

00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:11.400
in-jokes in Incredibles 2.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.469
When Dash says "We wanna fight bad guys!"

00:05:14.469 --> 00:05:16.669
"It defines who I am."

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:20.550
It's probably a reference to Christopher Nolan's
Batman origin movie "Batman Begins".

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:26.529
"It's not who I am underneath, but what I
do that defines me."

00:05:26.529 --> 00:05:30.389
Some of the visuals in the movie also felt
Batman-esque to me, such as how Elastigirl

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:34.840
looks like she's flying through the city like
a bat, and these rooftop shots of Elastigirl

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:40.250
which reminded me of Frank Miller's The Dark
Knight Returns and also Batman v Superman.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:44.839
Following Pixar tradition, Incredibles 2 includes
an easter egg to the studio's next movie,

00:05:44.839 --> 00:05:50.729
Toy Story 4; however, because details of Toy
Story 4 are still sparse at this point, Incredibles

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:55.150
2 sets supervisor Nathan Fariss has said that
the easter egg will probably only make sense

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.180
once Toy Story 4 comes out.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.830
I did spot what looks like a neon cowboy hat
during the helicopter rescue scene, which

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:06.949
could be a Woody easter egg or perhaps a reference
to a new Toy Story character or location.

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:09.879
If you saw anything else, do let me know in
the comments!

00:06:09.879 --> 00:06:13.699
The Incredibles 2 credits are a joy to watch,
but if you didn't quite stick around to the

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:17.830
very end then you may have missed a tiny little
teaser for a possible sequel.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:21.809
It's not a proper post-credits scene like
you might expect from a live-action superhero

00:06:21.809 --> 00:06:25.839
movie, but after all the credits have rolled
and just before the screen fades to black,

00:06:25.839 --> 00:06:28.649
a little image of the Underminer's drill pops
up again.

00:06:28.649 --> 00:06:33.069
This is a reminder that the villain got away
earlier in the movie and that he's still hanging

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:37.860
out underneath Municiberg ready to return
if Pixar want to greenlight an Incredibles

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:38.860
3.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:42.440
There's also a stack of secret cameos you
might have missed in the film.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:47.099
Singer-songwriter Usher makes a surprise cameo
in the movie as Frozone's biggest fan and

00:06:47.099 --> 00:06:51.810
his words in the sequel are also a call-back
to Syndrome's line to Mr Incredible in the

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:52.810
original movie.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.949
"I'm your number one fan!"

00:06:54.949 --> 00:06:59.069
"I mean, after all, I am your biggest fan."

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:03.099
This time around, Rick Dicker is voiced by
Breaking Bad and Better Call Saul actor Jonathan

00:07:03.099 --> 00:07:08.039
Banks as Bud Luckey, who previously voiced
the government agent, passed away between

00:07:08.039 --> 00:07:09.039
films.

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:12.939
John Ratzenberger, who's voiced a character
in every Pixar movie so far, returns as the

00:07:12.939 --> 00:07:14.599
voice of the Underminer.

00:07:14.599 --> 00:07:18.579
The sons of Incredibles director Brad Bird
lent their voices to the original movie and

00:07:18.579 --> 00:07:19.579
the sequel.

00:07:19.579 --> 00:07:23.569
Nick Bird, who was the voice of the tricycle
kid in the first movie, and also voiced Squirt

00:07:23.569 --> 00:07:27.059
in Finding Nemo, performed the voice of Monster
Jack-Jack.

00:07:27.059 --> 00:07:32.340
And Nick's elder brother, Michael Bird, returned
as the voice of Tony Rydinger, Violet's high-school

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.340
crush.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:38.409
Signage in the movie also provides a place
for loads of secret shout-outs for Pixar crew

00:07:38.409 --> 00:07:39.409
members.

00:07:39.409 --> 00:07:43.940
This Christov billboard advertising Bulgarian
overalls is a nod to Incredibles 2 art director

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.229
and Bulgarian-born Anthony Christov.

00:07:46.229 --> 00:07:50.129
Pixar legend Joe Ranft is honoured via the
Joe Ranft Grocery store.

00:07:50.129 --> 00:07:54.550
Ranft had an Oscar nomination as one of the
writers on Toy Story, and before passing away

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:59.370
he'd worked on all of Pixar's movies plus
voiced various characters.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:03.930
Incredibles 2 story manager Kathryn Hendrickson's
name appears on a sign on the side of a building

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.279
during the Underminer sequence.

00:08:06.279 --> 00:08:10.819
And current Technical Art Director at Pixar,
Michael Rutter, also gets a shout-out via

00:08:10.819 --> 00:08:14.539
the "Rutter's Everyday Repair and Replacement"
store in Municiberg.

00:08:14.539 --> 00:08:19.639
There's also a Domee's Deli named after Domee
Shi who directed the Pixar short Bao which

00:08:19.639 --> 00:08:21.800
plays in front of Incredibles 2.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:26.240
Now this next one would have been a delicious
easter egg for Incredibles fans everywhere.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.119
"Fifteen years too late."

00:08:29.119 --> 00:08:33.600
Incredibles 2 was originally going to be released
in 2019 meaning it would have come out 15

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.530
years after the original, making that Syndrome
line fit perfectly.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:42.330
Pixar though had numerous problems with Toy
Story 4 so the two films' release dates were

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:46.730
switched around, bringing the Incredibles
sequel forward one year – a bitter blow

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:48.490
for lovers of that Syndrome meme.

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:52.100
So, what other easter eggs or references did
you spot?

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:55.100
And did you enjoy the sequel as much as the
original movie?

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:58.990
Be sure to let me know and make sure you're
subscribed for a chance to win an Incredibles

00:08:58.990 --> 00:08:59.990
Funko Pop.

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:02.230
You can also tweet me any comments or requests.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:06.480
Coming up next are more Incredibles 2 videos
plus Jurassic World 2 and I'll link those

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.330
here on screen when they're ready.

